# Shopify vs repository (HARD — non-negotiable)

## Source of truth

**The repository is the Design System.** GitHub / **`shopify-build/`** owns sections, snippets, assets, CSS, JS, and docs.

Shopify is **only the runtime** for visual QA — never the master. Do **not** develop primarily in Theme Editor. Do **not** treat a remote theme as source of truth. Any fix found in QA → revise in **repo first**, then re-deploy to draft if needed.

**GitHub is NOT a review environment.** Andrew cannot approve sections from source/PR alone. Visual approval = Shopify preview (Theme Editor + storefront).

> I can't review a Shopify section from GitHub source files alone. Every completed section must be deployed to the Shopify development theme and accompanied by a preview URL. I will review the implementation visually in the Theme Editor and on the storefront (desktop + mobile), verify all settings and controls work correctly, then approve or request revisions. GitHub remains the source of truth for code, but visual approval happens in Shopify—not from the repository.

## Workflow

1. Build section in repository  
2. Commit (GitHub master)  
3. Deploy to Shopify **development / disposable draft** — **only** when Andrew names a theme ID in that same message (ask if missing; never invent)  
4. Send **preview URL** + Theme Editor URL for visual review  
5. Andrew reviews visually: desktop + mobile, spacing, type, breakpoints, settings/schema, TE controls, image crop, performance  
6. Revisions in repository → redeploy → repeat  
7. When approved: **freeze in repo**, then next section  
8. **Brian** pulls approved repo → production theme  

Draft themes are temporary and may be deleted anytime.

## Default: no Shopify mutations

Agents **MUST NOT** run `shopify theme push`, `pull`, `dev`, `delete`, publish, rename, or Theme Editor API changes unless Andrew **explicitly names a theme ID in that same message**.

